+
+# Scope and evidence
+
+Static review of main at `578b3657e744c1f6d13b57bcb010bd21bde873c2`, after
+PRs #952, #953 and #954 merged. The review covered core effect/worker state,
+shell dispatch and cleanup, workspace ownership, analyzer/plan/review/target,
+report/session/progress codecs, process/resource lifecycles and Python tool
+fallbacks. Existing open and recent closed Issues were checked on 2026-10-16.
+No implementation, runtime test, benchmark or Lean proof was executed during
+this audit. Earlier passing tests are not presented as fresh evidence.
+
+The software-design-philosophy review asks whether a change removes knowledge
+that callers currently have to duplicate. No compatibility adapter is required
+for proposed internal changes. Historical fixtures, corruption rejection,
+platform-specific behavior and independent completion evidence remain useful.
+
+# Assessment
+
+The inspected boundaries satisfy five of eight diagnostics: **6.25/10** using
+1.25 points per evidenced row. This is a qualitative scoped assessment, not a
+repository-wide numerical measurement or a comparison with earlier local scores.
+
+| Diagnostic | Result | Evidence or required improvement |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Modules have describable purposes | Pass | Core transitions, shell effects, analyzer discovery and artifact readers have explicit roles. |
+| Interfaces are simpler than implementations | Pass | Typed requests, verified plans and managed workspace APIs hide substantial machinery. |
+| Implementation changes remain local | Needs work | Output continuation knowledge spans the pending ledger and separate IDs/maps; cleanup classification spans workspace and shell. |
+| Interface comments describe intent | Pass | Process cancellation/drain, root lifecycle and typed request comments identify ownership and evidence limits. |
+| Design is reviewed with code | Pass | The recent issue-specific maintenance records and this review record concrete alternatives and rejected proposals. |
+| Important decisions have a single owner | Needs work | Cleanup classification is independently retained in two representations; output lifetime and continuation need synchronized registration. |
+| Module boundaries are discoverable | Pass | Existing architecture/runtime/selection/session concepts describe responsibilities; the findings concern internal ownership within these documented boundaries. |
+| 10–20% design-investment allocation | Unmeasured | No time-allocation evidence was collected; no point awarded. |
+
+The two proposed changes address the failed rows. Reaching an evidenced 10/10
+also requires measuring the investment criterion rather than assuming it from
+review activity. Improvement must reduce existing representations, not add a
+second generic state-management framework.
+
+# Proposed improvements
+
+## Output completion ownership
+
+[#955](https://github.com/HayaoSuzuki/hoimin/issues/955).
+
+`RunState.pending` owns effect lifetime, while `output_actions` and three
+singleton output IDs own continuation identity. `register`, acknowledgement,
+failure and retirement must coordinate these stores. An output pending record
+should own one explicit continuation, including a no-continuation variant for
+baseline output. Report-start acknowledgement history and terminal stop policy
+remain independent. This is a design burden; no production mismatch was
+observed.[^machine]
+
+Verification should preserve ordinary/synthetic start and finish ACKs, baseline
+no-op ACKs, diagnostic failure, final-report stop handling and late/duplicate
+completion rejection. Existing adversarial schedule and Lean correspondence
+tests are relevant. A representation-only change does not by itself require a
+new Lean state machine.
+
+## Internal cleanup result classification
+
+[#956](https://github.com/HayaoSuzuki/hoimin/issues/956).
+
+`CleanupRecord` stores report status and an independent retry reason; shell's
+`ManagedCleanupOutcome` repeats its result classification. Root cleanup emits
+Clean/Failed/Deferred, whereas the report status also includes Retained and
+CleanupAfterDelivery. An internal result can own its classification and attach
+retry information only to Deferred; report/lifecycle values should be
+projections. Remaining-root identity and cleanup-ready diagnostics retain their
+independent evidentiary meanings.[^owned][^shell]
+
+The current diagnostic-wording test can construct a Failed record inside a
+Deferred wrapper. That illustrates representational ambiguity, not a production
+failure. Replace the existing two layers; do not add a new pipeline. Preserve
+normal/outer execution/delivery paths and the bounded contention retry policy.
+
+# Candidates not raised
+
+- **Session `runs.finished`:** production decisions use ownership and `complete`,
+  but the real-CLI result-lifecycle oracle independently reads `finished` as
+  evidence that incomplete finalization ran. Ownership release and
+  `complete=false` also occur after a crash and are not equivalent evidence.
+  Removing the column would need new instrumentation or weaken an existing
+  check; simplification has not been demonstrated.[^session][^lifecycle-oracle]
+- **Start gate and cancellation protocol:** the two token types duplicate the
+  AtomicBool/Notify/Mutex protocol. They have independent admission/cancellation
+  semantics and must retain distinct instances and types. Private implementation
+  sharing is possible, but was deferred because wrapper cost and benefit are
+  smaller than the two ownership findings.[^process]
+- **Process drain flags and resource close flags:** process reap, output drain,
+  closed admission and completed cleanup are different observations, including
+  partial failure. No single derived state was established.[^process]
+- **Cleanup attempt versus safety/admission state:** local attempt history and
+  cleanup-ready durable-marker evidence are independent; neither can simply be
+  reconstructed from the other.[^shell][^owned]
+- **Plan discovery timeout string:** one consumer distinguishes a stable failure
+  code from two producers. A broad diagnostic-enum migration is disproportionate
+  to the current local coupling.[^plan]
+- **Analyzer callback, stored annotations and recording flag:** output strategy
+  and suppression during loop/finally revisits are independent. A single enum
+  would combine separate axes.[^analyzer]
+- **Ranking DTO fields, review history and filesystem classification:** these
+  serve saved explanation/validation, historical evidence and a pure-boundary
+  projection respectively. Their readers and existing validation contracts do
+  not establish redundant mutable ownership.
+- **Compatibility search:** old-schema migrations/field repair have already been
+  removed in recent changes. Opaque progress configuration, optional current
+  report fields, SQL corruption checks, current module/direct-script imports and
+  OS fallbacks were not classified as obsolete compatibility support.
+
+# Review record
+
+Investigation passes:
+
+1. Confirmed latest main and merged #949–951 fixes; compared open and recent
+   closed issue titles and relevant maintenance decisions.
+2. Traced production writers/readers, not just struct declarations or possible
+   arbitrary public construction.
+3. Followed ACK, failure, cancellation, retirement and cleanup timeout paths;
+   checked baseline no-continuation and delivery-specific report states.
+4. Separated cached projections, durable evidence and independent axes from
+   duplicate mutable decisions.
+5. Read existing tests/oracles; challenged and rejected the initial finished-column
+   deletion after discovering its independent real-process observation purpose.
+
+Proposal review passes:
+
+1. Bound each issue to an existing internal ownership boundary.
+2. Preserve external formats and separate report-only states from internal results.
+3. Specify behavioral tests rather than tests mirroring enum layout.
+4. Distinguish proposed changes, inspected behavior and unexecuted verification.
+5. Compare the net reduction with added abstractions; reject whole-machine and
+   generic pipeline rewrites.
+
+Publication review passes:
+
+1. Confirmed both Issue bodies use immutable links to the audited source revision.
+2. Rechecked open Issues immediately before creation; the two findings were absent.
+3. Verified each proposal explains its distinct scope from earlier completed Issues.
+4. An independent reader checked both drafts against production code and found
+   no blocking error; neither body claims a reproduced production failure.
+5. Read both published bodies back and compared them with the reviewed drafts;
+   both are open and match. Checked the final documentation diff for unrelated edits.
+
+Documentation validation passed: all 50 knowledge pages passed YAML/reserved-file,
+local-link and index-reachability checks; the new audit's eight source hashes and
+source IDs/footnotes matched. Strict documentation site generation and
+`git diff --check` passed. These checks validate this record, not runtime behavior.
